  "note": "Run on 2026-10-18 on the live AwwO, in the operator’s own account and workspace, on Claude Opus 4.8, every agent working in its own sandbox computer. A first copy of the canvas on Claude Opus 5.5 stopped within two minutes when the model provider limited its rate, so this copy ran on Claude Opus 4.8, as the other flagships did. Its first run finished 13 of 17 nodes: the Computer builder stopped on a model provider error. The second run ran it again with what it feeds; it also ran the session recorder again, and with it the three session agents, whose sessions are the ones the page replays. The tester fixed three failures in that page: every module read window.U, which the kernel never made; no module made U.views; and the library module had replaced the kernel’s U.library. The third run gave that to the seven builders other than the Kernel in their tasks and ran them again. In Chrome that page’s editor showed no code (no style covered the computer module’s views) and the library’s test counted a live session, so the fourth run ran the Computer and Library builders again with that in their tasks. In each of the last two runs the tester fixed two failures the kept modules bring back: the Take over command under two ids, and the self-test’s isolation of the library. The three sessions are real work done during the second run: each session agent ran every command, wrote every file, fetched every web address and looked at every page through the recorder (rec.py, which the Session recorder node writes from its template with no model), and the recorder wrote down what the computer printed. AwwO’s own log of those agents’ tool calls shows only shell commands, file reads and listings, and one publish each. Each node shows its last run, and every node’s text is the canvas a member copies today. The delivered page is the tester’s, exactly as delivered, with no manual edits.",
  "verification": "Before publishing, the delivered page was opened in Chrome (1440×900 and 1280×720) with every network request blocked. It opens replaying the bakery session at twice its speed: the plan as a checklist, the agent’s messages and a chip for each action in the thread, and the computer following each event (the editor as a file is written, the browser with the headless browser’s screenshots of the site at desktop and phone widths). Each recorded session plays to its end with its files, commands and what they printed: the CO2 session’s eight commands, its chart and report.md (1959 averaged 315.98 ppm, 2025 427.35 ppm); the earthquake session’s USGS requests, its check of the top three against their own records, and quakes.md. A click on a chip seeks to that event and opens its file. Take over hands the computer to the person (ls -l and echo into a file ran in its terminal), and Hand back returns to the replay. A live task after Connect says in this preview that the page cannot reach the network and works when opened on its own. Its self-test passes all 51 tests and rejects all 22 planted failures, as the tester reported, and leaves the page as it was, after a live session too. One fault was seen: at a phone’s 390 px width the top bar and the timeline run past the screen’s edge."
